.TH SAPHIRA-BUILD 8 "September 2026" "Saphira Linux" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
saphira-build \- Saphira package builder daemon and queue
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B saphira-build
.B scan
|
.B queue
|
.B status
|
.B run
.RB [ --limit= N ]
|
.B retry
.I PACKAGE
|
.B log
.I PACKAGE
|
.B publish
.RI [ PKG ...]
|
.B daemon
.RB [ --once ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B saphira-build
drives the Saphira package pipeline:
.B scan
detects recipes ahead of the repository,
.B run
builds them rootless through
.BR buildpkg (1),
successful builds land as signed,
indexed packages via
.B publish
(selective ownership-gated publication), and a bare
.BR sign-apk-repo (8)
run remains the slow full-audit reconciler.
.PP
Builds run rootless; signing, publication and controller refresh
are privileged admin boundaries (sudo), alongside
.BR installpkg (8).
.SS Fresh-machine bring-up
A new build machine needs no tree surgery: install the controller
and tooling from the repository, clone the recipes, enable the
daemon.
.SS Enablement is host state, and that is the whole failure mode
The package deliberately does NOT enable or start its own service.
A shipped unit or init script is inert until an operator or an image
profile enables it, and which init is live is never inferred at
install time. So enablement is per-host state, not payload, and a
controller that was started by hand and never enabled returns from a
reboot with nothing running and no error: the queue database is
intact, so every query still answers, which is exactly what makes the
outage silent.
.PP
A controller expected to survive reboots belongs in the
.B default
runlevel:
.PP
.RS
sudo rc-update add saphira-build default
.br
sudo rc-service saphira-build start
.RE
.PP
After a reboot, check rather than assume:
.PP
.RS
rc-update show default | grep saphira-build
.br
rc-service saphira-build status
.RE
.PP
An empty
.B rc-update show default
alongside a populated
.I /var/lib/saphira-build/queue.db
means the queue is intact and idle. It is not evidence that builds are
running, and it is not evidence that they are hung; the queue is a
record of attempts, so it answers just as well when nothing has run
for days.
.SS Controller self-refresh (Egg only)
The controller host rebuilds the builder with itself, so deploying
controller fixes needs one explicit refresh: install the tree tools
over
.IR /usr/bin ,
restart the daemon (it holds the old code in memory), then retry
the failed package. This loop is Egg-only pathology. Fresh machines
never need it: their first
.B installpkg
already carries current code.
.SH OPTIONS
Subcommands take no general options; see SYNOPSIS for per-command
arguments.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/saphira-build
Deployed controller (refreshed from the tree when fixed).
.TP
.I /var/lib/saphira-build/queue.db
Build queue and results (controller state; never repository metadata).
.TP
.I /out/stage4/incoming/
Unsigned
.I *-ready
transactions awaiting publication.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Command completed.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid invocation, failed build step, or daemon error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
sudo rc-update add saphira-build default
.br
sudo rc-service saphira-build start
.br
saphira-build status
.br
saphira-build retry nginx
.br
saphira-build log nginx
.RE
